 "mismatches": [
  {
   "entry": "lobo2024impact#2",
   "source": "lanham2023measuring",
   "finding": "Attributed to Lanham et al., who never define a faithful CoT as one whose steps logically lead to the answer rather than a predetermined conclusion; they state the opposite: \"Reasoning not being post-hoc does not guarantee faithfulness, nor does being post-hoc exclude faithfulness.\" Nearest: \"Since post-hoc reasoning does not change the model’s answer, there is no strong reason to believe that such reasoning would be faithful.\""
  },
  {
   "entry": "hong2026making#1",
   "source": "chen2025reasoning",
   "finding": "\"Parametric faithfulness\" does not occur in Chen et al. 2025 (nor in Hase & Potts 2026, also cited); nearest is \"The CoT $c$ is faithful if it accurately reflects $M$ ’s internal reasoning process\". The term is Tutek et al. 2025's, meaning that unlearning a step changes the answer; hong2026making#5 applies it to the hint test, which Tutek et al. class as contextual."
  },
  {
   "entry": "imrankhan2026better#2",
   "source": "turpin2023language",
   "finding": "Turpin et al. 2023 define no Unfaithful CoT Rate; their metrics are the accuracy drop in biased contexts, \"% Unfaithful Overall\" (fraction of prediction pairs that change when the biasing feature is added) and \"Percentage of Unfaithfulness Explained by Bias\"; verbalization was checked by hand (1 of 426 explanations)."
  },
  {
   "entry": "pmlr-v235-chen24bl#6",
   "source": "jacovi2020towards",
   "finding": "Plausibility \"based on its factual correctness and logical coherence\" is credited partly to Jacovi & Goldberg, who define it only as \"how convincing the interpretation is to humans\"; the same sentence recurs in chen2025consistent#3 and chen2025reasoning#6. Not settled whether the wording comes from the co-cited Herman 2017 or Lage et al. 2019 (outside the map)."
  },
  {
   "entry": "sanyal2022fairr#2",
   "source": "jacovi2020towards",
   "finding": "Jacovi & Goldberg never frame faithfulness as whether models learn the task rather than shortcut patterns; nearest is \"a faithful interpretation is one that accurately represents the reasoning process behind the model’s prediction\"."
  },
  {
   "entry": "hong2026making#2",
   "source": "parcalabescu2023measuring",
   "finding": "Parcalabescu & Frank's self-consistency is output-level agreement between explanation and prediction, tested by \"self-consistency tests that compare the outputs of models after modifying their inputs\" or by CC-SHAP; consistency across multiple samples or under paraphrase is not stated. Co-cited Zhao & Iii 2025 (outside the map) not checked."
  },
  {
   "entry": "li2025drift#3",
   "source": "parcalabescu2023measuring",
   "finding": "Cites Parcalabescu & Frank for perturbation-based faithfulness evaluation, the view they argue against: \"these faithfulness tests do not measure faithfulness to the models’ inner workings – but rather their self-consistency at output level.\""
  },
  {
   "entry": "hoth2026atmanrl#5",
   "source": "agarwal2024faithfulness",
   "finding": "Agarwal et al. state the plausible-but-unfaithful contrast (\"responses that seem logical and coherent (i.e., being plausible)... may not accurately reflect the model’s actual reasoning process\") but say nothing about shortcuts that bypass the stated reasoning."
  },
  {
   "entry": "haskins2026training#3",
   "source": "emmons2025chain",
   "finding": "Emmons et al. do not use \"faithfulness floor\"; nearest: \"for sufficiently difficult tasks, such as complex sabotage, CoT-as-computation becomes necessary\"."
  },
  {
   "entry": "yang2025investigating#6",
   "source": "turpin2025teaching",
   "finding": "The formula (1 − verbalization rate) × cue influence rate is Turpin et al. 2025's, but they call it \"the effective cue influence rate (ECR)\", not Scheming."
  }
